          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The helmet signifies God-given salvation—especially assurance or hope of final salvation—which protects the believer in spiritual conflict, often understood as guarding the mind against doubt and despair. The divine-warrior background is a recognized secondary framing.\",\"book_summary\":\"The helmet is salvation covering the head, understood through Christ as the Head. It is God’s own armor given to his people and protects the bodily-symbolic location where allegiance to God or a counterfeit power is marked.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"God’s salvation or hope of salvation functioning as protective armor for the believer.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"God’s own salvific armor covering the believer’s head, with the head related to Christ and the site of allegiance marking.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains salvation as protective armor but shifts the specific protected domain from the consensus emphasis on the believer’s mind, confidence, or hope to a typological complex involving Christ as Head and the forehead as the location of true or counterfeit allegiance. Thus cases interpreted merely as psychological assurance are deemphasized or reclassified, while allegiance-marking imagery is added.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction at the basic level: both identify the helmet with salvation supplied by God and functioning protectively. The disagreement concerns what the protected head specifically signifies and how the helmet’s function should be delimited.\",\"rationale\":\"The central referent is preserved rather than replaced: the book still identifies the helmet as salvation and explicitly adopts the recognized divine-warrior interpretation that God lends his own armor to believers. Its Christ-as-Head and allegiance-marker claims materially specify a different symbolic boundary than the mainstream mind/assurance emphasis, making the entry refined rather than a simple match. It is not novel because the consensus already includes objective salvation, divine-warrior armor, and Christ-centered readings.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Isaiah 59, Ephesians 6, and 1 Thessalonians 5 strongly support salvation and God’s armor, but they do not themselves identify the helmet’s protected location with allegiance marking. First Corinthians 11 identifies Christ as head and Revelation 22 places God’s name on foreheads, yet joining those texts into the book’s specific helmet symbolism is inferential; moreover, the cited texts do not explicitly supply the counterfeit-allegiance half of the claim.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
